    "q-129633": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129633",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-2,0)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(-2,0)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(-2,0)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,0),$ estimate the value of $f(-1.99,0.01).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,0)+∇f(-2,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-2),y-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(-2,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-2,0),{f}_{y}(-2,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,y⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.99,0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1.99$ and $y=0.01,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.99,0.01) & ≈-3+⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1.99+2,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3+1(0.01)+0(0.01) \\\\  & =-3+0.01+0 \\\\  & =-2.99\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
