    "q-98327": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98327",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(3,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(3,-1)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,-0.97).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(3,-1)+∇f(3,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,-1),{f}_{y}(3,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.01,-0.97)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01$ and $y=-0.97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,-0.97) & ≈-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,-0.97+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =-3-0.01+0.03 \\\\  & =-2.98\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
