    "q-129661": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129661",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(0,1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,-1)+∇f(0,1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-1,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,-1),{f}_{y}(0,1,-1),{f}_{z}(0,1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,1,-1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,-1) & =(-1){e}^{0}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,1,-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,1,-1) & ={e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.01,y=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.01,1.01,-0.99) & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2-1(0.01)-1(0.01)+1(0.01) \\\\  & ≈-2-0.01-0.01+0.01 \\\\  & ≈-2.01\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
