    "q-129654": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129654",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,-1)+∇f(1,0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,0,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,-1),{f}_{y}(1,0,-1),{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02,$ $y=0.1,$ and $z=-0.96$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,0.1,-0.96) & ≈1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1.02-1,0.1,-0.96+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =1-1(0.02)-1(0.1)+0(0.04) \\\\  & =1-0.02-0.1+0 \\\\  & =0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
