    "q-98347": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98347",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(3,0,4)=25, &  & {f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, &  & {f}_{z}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,0.1,3.99).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,0,4)+∇f(3,0,4)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-0,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨{f}_{x}(3,0,4),{f}_{y}(3,0,4),{f}_{z}(3,0,4)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,0,4)$, ${f}_{y}(3,0,4)$ and ${f}_{z}(3,0,4),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(0)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=0 \\\\ {f}_{z}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(4)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.01,0.1,3.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01,y=0.1$ and $z=3.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,0.1,3.99) & ≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,0.1,3.99-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =25+0.03-0.04 \\\\  & =24.99\\end{aligned}\n$$",
