      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1,-1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+(x-1)-(y+1)-(z+1) \\\\  & ≈-2+x-y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
